<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ai-Khanoum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 300–145 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ai-Khanoum is the site of a Hellenistic city at the meeting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> and Kokcha rivers in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, probably founded in the early Seleucid period around 300 BCE. Excavated by French archaeologists from 1964 to 1978, it revealed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a>, palace and Greek inscriptions, making it the best-known city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom</a>. It was abandoned around 145 BCE after nomad invasions, and the site was heavily looted during the Afghan wars.</p></div>
